8250: CE5300 GEN3 UARTs are timer-polled — drive TX from the LSR

The Intel CE5300 (EA family) declares IRQ 38 for its SoC UARTs, and on Control4's
stock 3.12 kernel that interrupt fires (/proc/interrupts "38: ... serial" climbs,
ttyS1's IO-MCU has non-zero tx AND rx). Under openHC the identical declaration
registers but never fires — measured: IRQ 38 sits at 0 across a full transmit —
so the ports are run timer-polled instead (patch 0002 declares irq 0, which makes
serial8250 arm serial8250_timeout() and call handle_irq from a timer).

Polled RX already works: when the IO-MCU answers, the received-data condition is
reflected in the IIR, so serial8250_handle_irq() proceeds and rx_chars() runs.
Polled TX does not. The GEN3/XScale block does not reflect the THRE (TX FIFO
empty) condition in the IIR — the same silicon quirk Control4 works around in its
own interrupt handler — so on a poll tick with a byte queued the IIR reads
UART_IIR_NO_INT and serial8250_handle_irq() takes its early return before ever
looking at the LSR. The TX FIFO is never refilled: the ring buffer drains to the
driver, tx counts up, and nothing reaches the wire (the IO-MCU stays silent,
rx=0, gpio-ohc-iomcu never registers a gpiochip/lirc/rc).

serial8250_handle_irq_locked() already drives both RX and TX straight from the
LSR (it checks UART_LSR_DR and UART_LSR_THRE, not the IIR). The only thing in the
way is the IIR early return. For a polled XScale port (irq == 0, type set by
patch 0006) fall through to it instead, so every timer tick services TX as well
as RX. Interrupt-driven ports are untouched: the guard requires irq == 0.

Scope: EA family only (board/ea-common/patches). Pairs with 0002 (irq 0) and
0006 (PORT_XSCALE).

Author: Joseph Telaak <joseph@telaak.dev>
---
--- a/drivers/tty/serial/8250/8250_port.c
+++ b/drivers/tty/serial/8250/8250_port.c
@@ -1857,7 +1857,18 @@
  */
 int serial8250_handle_irq(struct uart_port *port, unsigned int iir)
 {
-	if (iir & UART_IIR_NO_INT)
+	/*
+	 * Intel CE5300 (EA family) runs its SoC UARTs timer-polled — see patch
+	 * 0002. IRQ 38 is declared but never fires under openHC, so the ports are
+	 * given irq 0 and serviced by serial8250_timeout(). The GEN3/XScale block
+	 * also does not reflect the THRE (TX-empty) condition in the IIR, so this
+	 * UART_IIR_NO_INT early return would leave the TX FIFO unfilled and
+	 * transmission would hang (tx counts up, nothing reaches the IO-MCU, rx
+	 * stays 0). For a polled XScale port fall through instead and let
+	 * serial8250_handle_irq_locked() drive RX and TX from the LSR every tick.
+	 */
+	if ((iir & UART_IIR_NO_INT) &&
+	    !(port->irq == 0 && port->type == PORT_XSCALE))
 		return 0;
 
 	guard(uart_port_lock_check_sysrq_irqsave)(port);
